<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadu Amadu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Amadu III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadu Amadu was the third and last ruler of the Massina Empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> Islamic state in the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, from 1853. Grandson of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>, he faced internal opposition and the rivalry of the Tijani leader Umar Tall. In 1862 Umar's army defeated him and took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>, and Amadu Amadu was captured and executed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadu Amadu, also called Amadu III, was born in 1830, the son of Amadu II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a>, who nominated him as his successor. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> had launched a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the Fulbe chiefs of Massina, tributaries of the Bambara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segu</a>, and built an empire ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a> by a council of forty elders under a strict Islamic theocracy. In 1853 Amadu III was elected almami in accordance with his father's wishes, passing over another candidate, his uncle Ba Lobbo, who stayed influential as an army leader and a member of the Tijani faction at court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Less learned than his father and grandfather, Amadu kept their puritanical practices, such as banning tobacco and requiring full segregation of men and women, but was more willing to follow Fulbe social traditions. Tensions grew between the stricter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijaniyya">Tijaniyya</a> minority and the more relaxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a>. When the German traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in September 1853, the Kunta leader Ahmad al-Bakkay refused Amadu's demands to hand him over, and Amadu ordered Barth's death as an infidel. He raided deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segu</a> territory until 1855.</p><p>After defeating Kaarta in 1855, the Tukolor leader al-Hajj Umar Tall proposed an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Segu</a>, but Amadu instead sent an army against him, which was defeated at Kassakeri in 1856. When Umar attacked Segu in 1861, Amadu aided its king Bina Ali on condition that he accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Ba Lobbo led 8,000 cavalry, 5,000 infantry and 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> to Tio, opposite Sinsani, where Umar crushed the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> and Segu before taking Segu itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Criticised for making war on Muslims, Umar argued that Bina Ali's conversion was a sham and that Amadu had taken a thousand mithqals of gold to support him. In 1862 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a>, meeting little resistance; the Kunta under al-Bakkay now joined Amadu, holding the attack unlawful. Amadu refused Umar's offer of a duel and was defeated on 15 May 1862 at Cayawal, then captured and beheaded. Ba Lobbo resisted briefly, but the Massina Empire ceased to be an effective force. A revolt against Umar broke out in 1863, and he was killed in 1864.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadu III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Amadu Seku and grandson <b>Amadu Amadu</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/state/massina-empire">Massina Empire</a></li></ul></section>
